WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.680
One bacterium and one virus land in a warm flask full of food.

2
00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:11.220
An hour later, which has made more of itself?

3
00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:13.740
And how did it do it?

4
00:00:14.683 --> 00:00:16.263
Start with the bacterium.

5
00:00:17.343 --> 00:00:21.323
It is a single cell, with its DNA in one loop called the chromosome.

6
00:00:22.803 --> 00:00:24.543
There is no nucleus around it.

7
00:00:25.433 --> 00:00:27.473
First the cell copies its chromosome.

8
00:00:28.773 --> 00:00:31.573
Then it grows longer, with one copy at each end.

9
00:00:32.753 --> 00:00:35.093
Then it pinches in the middle and splits in two.

10
00:00:36.117 --> 00:00:37.657
This is binary fission.

11
00:00:38.957 --> 00:00:41.837
Each new cell gets a complete copy of the chromosome.

12
00:00:43.157 --> 00:00:46.297
So the two cells match, except for rare copying mistakes.

13
00:00:47.417 --> 00:00:49.717
Now each new cell does the same thing.

14
00:00:50.837 --> 00:00:55.437
One becomes two. Two become four, then eight, then sixteen.

15
00:00:56.877 --> 00:00:58.337
Every round doubles the count.

16
00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:02.360
The time for one round is called the doubling time.

17
00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:08.420
In the best lab conditions, an E. coli cell can double about every 20 minutes.

18
00:01:09.533 --> 00:01:12.953
Keep that pace for seven hours, with no cells dying.

19
00:01:14.253 --> 00:01:18.253
Will one cell become closer to a thousand cells, or closer to a million?

20
00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:26.000
More than a million.

21
00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:31.060
The counter reaches 21 doublings, about two million cells.

22
00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:35.580
That is a calculated number, not a count of real cells.

23
00:01:36.683 --> 00:01:38.463
But a real flask is closed.

24
00:01:39.703 --> 00:01:42.183
As the cells multiply, they use up the food.

25
00:01:43.483 --> 00:01:45.743
Their wastes build up in the liquid around them.

26
00:01:46.717 --> 00:01:49.477
So the climb slows, and the count levels off.

27
00:01:50.897 --> 00:01:54.937
Doubling makes numbers huge, but only while food lasts and wastes stay low.

28
00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:01.600
Now the virus. This one is a phage, a virus that infects bacteria.

29
00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:05.280
Left alone in all that food, what does it do?

30
00:02:06.250 --> 00:02:09.350
Nothing. It does not grow.

31
00:02:10.610 --> 00:02:11.610
It does not split.

32
00:02:12.770 --> 00:02:14.570
It cannot make anything on its own.

33
00:02:15.830 --> 00:02:17.190
Its counter stays at one.

34
00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:21.460
Then it lands on a bacterium and injects its DNA.

35
00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:25.900
Soon, orange phage parts start to appear inside the cell.

36
00:02:26.933 --> 00:02:28.793
The phage is not making them.

37
00:02:30.053 --> 00:02:35.513
The cell is. The cell reads the phage genes as if they were its own, and builds

38
00:02:35.513 --> 00:02:36.513
phage parts.

39
00:02:37.283 --> 00:02:39.863
So the two multiply in opposite ways.

40
00:02:41.023 --> 00:02:43.343
The bacterium grows and splits on its own.

41
00:02:44.543 --> 00:02:46.003
The virus never divides.

42
00:02:47.203 --> 00:02:49.123
A host cell builds every new virus.

43
00:02:50.167 --> 00:02:52.647
Here is the one rule to keep from this film.

44
00:02:53.947 --> 00:02:55.487
A bacterium copies itself.

45
00:02:56.707 --> 00:02:59.807
A virus is copied by a host cell, never by itself.
